*Selection sort selects the minimum element from the unssorted array repeatedly and swaps it with first element
Algorithm :
For an array of size n
1. Start from index 0.
2. Assume the current index contains the minimum.
3. Search the remaining unsorted elements.
4. Find the actual minimum element.
5. Swap it with the element at the current index.
6. Move to the next index.
7. Repeat until the array is sorted.

Program:
public static void selectionSort(int[] nums) {
  int n = nums.length;
  for(int i=0;i<n;i++) {
    int minIndex = i;
    for(int j=i+1;j<n;j++) {
      if(nums[j] < nums[minIndex]) {
        minIndex = j;
      }
    }
    int temp  = nums[i];
    nums[i] = nums[minIndex];
    nums[minIndex] = temp;
  }
  return nums;
 }


Given an array of integers nums, sort the array in non-decreasing order using the selection sort algorithm and return the sorted array.
A sorted array in non-decreasing order is an array where each element is greater than or equal to all previous elements in the array.


Example 1

Input: nums = [7, 4, 1, 5, 3]

Output: [1, 3, 4, 5, 7]


Time Complexity
Case	Complexity	Why
Best case	O(n²)	Still searches the remaining array every pass
Average case	O(n²)	Same comparisons
Worst case	O(n²)	Same comparisons

Space = O(1)
